problems.description_md:
A spreadsheet row can hold cells of different kinds. One vector can hold them all because each element is the same enum type (Book ch. 8.1, "Using an Enum to Store Multiple Types"). The starter defines `pub enum Cell { Number(i32), Text(String), Empty }`.

`row(cells)` describes the borrowed row as one line of text. Each cell becomes one field:

- `Number(n)` becomes `N:` followed by `n` in decimal (with a `-` sign if negative);
- `Text(t)` becomes `T:` followed by `t`, copied exactly (no trimming or escaping, even if `t` contains `|`);
- `Empty` becomes `-`.

The fields are joined with a single `|` between neighbours, with no `|` at the start or end and no newline. An empty row gives an empty string. The function only reads the row: the caller still owns the vector and its `String`s afterwards.

starter.text:
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub enum Cell {
    Number(i32),
    Text(String),
    Empty,
}

pub fn row(cells: &[Cell]) -> String {
    String::new()
}

solution.text:
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub enum Cell {
    Number(i32),
    Text(String),
    Empty,
}

pub fn row(cells: &[Cell]) -> String {
    let mut out = String::new();
    let mut first = true;
    for cell in cells {
        if !first {
            out.push('|');
        }
        first = false;
        match cell {
            Cell::Number(n) => out.push_str(&format!("N:{n}")),
            Cell::Text(t) => {
                out.push_str("T:");
                out.push_str(t);
            }
            Cell::Empty => out.push('-'),
        }
    }
    out
}

