problems.description_md:
`window(text, start, end)` returns `Some(&text[start..end])` when that slice is valid, and `None` otherwise. It must never panic.

String slices use **byte** positions. The Book (ch. 8.2, "Slicing Strings") warns that `&text[start..end]` panics if either end falls inside a multi-byte character, or if the range is reversed or past the end. A slice is valid only when all of these hold:

- `start <= end`;
- `end <= text.len()`;
- both `start` and `end` are character boundaries (`text.is_char_boundary(i)`; positions 0 and `text.len()` always are).

The result borrows from `text` (it is part of the input, not a new allocation), so the return type is `Option<&str>`. An empty window such as `start == end` on a boundary is valid and gives `Some("")`.

starter.text:
pub fn window(text: &str, start: usize, end: usize) -> Option<&str> {
    None
}

solution.text:
pub fn window(text: &str, start: usize, end: usize) -> Option<&str> {
    if start <= end
        && end <= text.len()
        && text.is_char_boundary(start)
        && text.is_char_boundary(end)
    {
        Some(&text[start..end])
    } else {
        None
    }
}

